INSERT INTO red_pill_verses (verse_id, claim_class, class_label, card_title, card_md, anchor_refs, tier_required, last_reviewed_at)
VALUES ((SELECT v.id FROM verses v JOIN chapters c ON c.id = v.chapter_id JOIN books b ON b.id = c.book_id JOIN editions e ON e.id = b.edition_id WHERE e.slug = 'canon' AND b.slug = 'mark' AND c.chapter_number = 12 AND v.verse_number = 36), 'structural', 'Only possible if', 'Yahuah (LORD) said unto my Lord', $card$*And Yahusha (Jesus) answered and said, while he taught in the temple, How say the scribes that Messiah (Christ) is the Son of David? For David himself said by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), Yahuah (LORD) said to my Lord, Sit thou on my right hand, till I make thine enemies thy footstool. David therefore himself calleth him Yahuah (Lord); and whence is he then his son? And the common people heard him gladly.* (Mark 12:35-37)

*Yahuah (LORD) said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool.* (Psalm 110:1)

His own riddle to the scribes, left hanging in the temple air. David, king of Yashar'el (Israel), bows to no man — yet by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) he calls someone *my Lord,* seated at the right hand of Yahuah (LORD). Whom does a king call Lord? Not a son who is merely a son. The psalm holds two — Yahuah (LORD) who speaks, and David's Lord who sits — the Formless and the Formed, in one verse, a thousand years early. *And the common people heard him gladly.*$card$, '{"Psalm 110:1"}'::TEXT[], 'free', NOW())
